The paper also considers attention written with a similarity function and then factorized through feature maps $\phi$ and $\varphi$. Here $\phi(\boldsymbol{q}_m)$ and $\varphi(\boldsymbol{k}_n)$ are typically nonnegative transformed features. Its RoPE variant rotates those features in the numerator:

$$
\frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}
(\boldsymbol{R}_{\Theta,m}^{d}\phi(\boldsymbol{q}_m))^{\intercal}
(\boldsymbol{R}_{\Theta,n}^{d}\varphi(\boldsymbol{k}_n))\boldsymbol{v}_n}
{\sum_{n=1}^{N}\phi(\boldsymbol{q}_m)^{\intercal}\varphi(\boldsymbol{k}_n)}.
$$

The denominator is deliberately left unchanged. Since rotated numerator terms can be negative, the resulting coefficients are not necessarily a probability distribution: they need not all be nonnegative even though the denominator normalizes the unrotated feature interaction. Therefore “attention weight” here should not automatically be interpreted as a categorical probability.

The paper argues that this construction retains the factorized computation associated with linear attention and reports a Performer-based experiment under its stated settings. Those results establish empirical behavior in that setting, not a general guarantee for every linear-attention feature map, architecture, or dataset.
